title: Nutanix Prism: Protection Policies
agents: nutanix
catalog: virtual/nutanix
license: GPLv2
distribution: check_mk
description:
 This check monitors the protection policies a Nutanix Prism Central owns,
 with the v4 API. It shows the policy's recovery point objective (RPO), the
 number of VMs it protects, its remote locations and the retention of each
 schedule. Policies of another Prism Central that replicate to this one are
 monitored on the Prism Central owning them.

 For each protected VM it checks the age of the VM's newest recovery point.
 The levels are multiples of the policy's shortest RPO other than 0 (an RPO
 of 0 is synchronous replication) and can be configured with the
 rule "Nutanix protection policies". By default the service goes {WARN} at
 1.5 times and {CRIT} at 2 times the RPO. With several schedules, e.g.
 hourly local recovery points and a daily replication to a remote site, the
 levels follow the shortest RPO and the age counts from the newest recovery
 point of any schedule. The check doesn't notice if only the longer schedule
 fails. A VM without any recovery point
 gets the same levels, counted from when the check first saw it. Nutanix
 takes a VM's first recovery point as soon as it protects the VM, so a newly
 protected VM usually has one at once. A manual recovery point counts like
 one made by the policy's schedule, because the API doesn't tell them apart.
 A VM whose recovery point the agent couldn't fetch is {UNKNOWN}.

 For VMs of policies that replicate to a remote location synchronously or
 every 1 to 15 minutes, it shows the replication state of each VM per remote
 location: in sync, syncing or out of sync. The v4 API reports no replication
 state for hourly or longer schedules, so the check shows none for them.
 Out of sync means the replication schedule is disabled and nothing
 replicates. By default out of sync is {WARN}, the other states are {OK}. A
 schedule with paused replication (only possible for synchronous replication)
 is {WARN}, if Prism Central reports it: the v4.0 API documents no such
 field, but Prism Central 7.5 sends it.
 A VM whose replication state the agent couldn't fetch is {UNKNOWN}.
 If Prism Central reports no replication state for such a VM, the check
 says so in the details and stays {OK}.

 VMs on ESXi clusters are not covered: the v4 API shows which policy protects
 a VM only for AHV VMs. A policy protecting only ESXi VMs shows "Protected
 VMs: 0".

 The v4 API can't read protection domains. Convert them to protection
 policies to monitor them with this check.

 This check requires the special agent configurable through "Nutanix Prism"
 with the v4 API.

item:
 The name of the protection policy and the last 8 characters of its ID in
 brackets, e.g. "daily (1a2b3c4d)".

discovery:
 One service is created for each protection policy the Prism Central owns.
